\f3\b\fs62 \cf2 The Expensive Mistakes Behind Weak Agent Architectures\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs34 \cf2 \cb3 Building a graph before understanding the work
\f1\b0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 Teams sometimes translate a business process into dozens of nodes before they have observed how a capable agent actually solves it. Start with traces from a simpler harness, then formalize the stable paths.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf2 \cb3 Letting the same model write and grade without safeguards
\f1\b0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 Self-review can help, but it is vulnerable to shared blind spots. Prefer deterministic checks where possible, separate reviewer context, and require human approval for high-impact actions.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf2 \cb3 Using "keep trying" as a loop specification
\f1\b0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 An unbounded retry loop is a cost leak. Every loop needs a measurable objective, fresh evidence, maximum attempts and a named escalation path.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf2 \cb3 Treating the harness as a dumping ground
\f1\b0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 More tools and memory are not automatically better. A crowded toolset raises selection errors, a noisy context raises confusion, and broad permissions raise risk.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf2 \cb3 Blaming the model for orchestration failures
\f1\b0 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb3 A model cannot compensate reliably for stale state, ambiguous tool schemas, broken APIs or missing exit conditions. Improve the layer that owns the failure.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f2\b\fs52 \cf2 \cb3 A production-ready design checklist
\f4 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0
\f2\fs34 \cf2 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Harness:
\f1\b0  Are tools narrow, documented and observable? Is state durable? Are permissions least-privilege? Can operators pause, inspect and resume a run?\cb1 \
\ls7\ilvl0
\f2\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Loop:
\f1\b0  What evidence proves success? What feedback is returned on failure? How many retries are allowed? What happens when the budget is exhausted?\cb1 \
\ls7\ilvl0
\f2\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Graph:
\f1\b0  Which paths must be deterministic? Where can work run in parallel? Which state is shared? Where are the human gates and recovery routes?\cb1 \
\ls7\ilvl0
\f2\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Evaluation:
\f1\b0  Can the team replay real traces, compare versions and attribute improvement to a specific change rather than intuition?\cb1 \
\ls7\ilvl0
\f2\b \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Operations:
\f1\b0  Are cost, latency, failure rate, intervention rate and task-level success monitored in production?\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f4\b\fs52 \cf2 \cb3 The Simplest Way to Remember the Difference\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f1\b0\fs34 \cf2 \cb3 Engineering something to be a model to make it operate. Loop engineering methodology is iterative, verifiable and resumable. A complex execution path is made explicit and controllable through the use of graph engineering. None of the three is substituted by any of the others. Even if the harness has lost its state, a beautifully drawn graph is no sufficient. However, even with the best harness, if there is no evidence or stop rule, it is a waste of money! Carefully crafted loops are still hard to operate when branching, parallelism and approvals are embedded in the ad-hoc code. If these three layers are designed jointly, reliable agent systems will arise, provided that the team is aware of what each layer is supposed to solve\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

